Question: What must national law let local-network operators do, and what must it give them, when they would rather purchase services from nearby generators, storage or demand response than upgrade their lines?

Answer: The passages do not answer the question for local-network (distribution) operators. The closest text, in Article 40, concerns transmission system operators. It says the regulatory framework must ensure they can procure services from demand response or energy storage providers, and must promote energy efficiency measures where these services cost-effectively avoid upgrading or replacing capacity. Article 36 only covers when distribution operators may own or operate storage. Nothing here states what national law must let distribution operators do, or what it must give them, when they procure local flexibility instead of reinforcing their networks.

