Question: What must national law let local-network operators do, and what must it give them, when they would rather purchase services from nearby generators, storage or demand response than upgrade their lines?

Answer: The passages do not answer this question. They contain no provision on what national law must let distribution system operators do, or give them, when they prefer to buy flexibility services from local generation, storage or demand response instead of reinforcing their grids. What they do state is narrower. Directive 2019/944 Article 17(2) requires that TSOs and DSOs procuring ancillary services treat demand response aggregators non-discriminatorily alongside producers, based on technical capabilities. Regulation 2019/943 Article 13 requires redispatching to be open to all generation, storage and demand response, selected by market-based mechanisms and compensated. Neither sets out DSO rights or incentives to procure flexibility as an alternative to network upgrades.

