Question: A wind farm is switched off by the grid operator without a market mechanism. Is the owner compensated?

Answer: Generally yes. Under Article 13(7), non-market-based redispatching (which includes curtailment per Article 2(26)) is subject to financial compensation paid by the system operator requesting it to the operator of the redispatched facility. The exception is producers that accepted a connection agreement with no guarantee of firm delivery of energy. The compensation must be at least the higher of (a) the additional operating cost caused by the redispatching, or (b) the net revenues from day-ahead market sales the facility would have earned without the request, including financial support that would have been received. A combination of both may be used if applying only the higher would give an unjustifiably low or high result.

