Question: A wind farm is switched off by the grid operator without a market mechanism. Is the owner compensated?

Answer: Generally yes. Under Article 13(7), where non-market-based redispatching is used (which includes downward redispatching of a wind farm), the system operator requesting it must financially compensate the operator of the redispatched facility. The compensation must be at least equal to the higher of (a) the additional operating costs caused by the redispatching, or (b) the net revenues from day-ahead market sales the facility would have made without the request, including any volume-based financial support it would have received. A combination of both may be used if applying only the higher would be unjustifiably low or high. The exception is producers that accepted a connection agreement with no guarantee of firm delivery of energy; they are not entitled to this compensation.

